<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potnia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Potnia, meaning 'Mistress' or 'Lady', is a title of a goddess or goddesses recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets of Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, from Knossos, Pylos and other palaces, often with an epithet such as 'Potnia of the labyrinth' or 'Potnia of the horses'. She received offerings of oil, honey and other goods, and the word survived as an epithet of Greek goddesses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potnia.jpg" alt="Potnia" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lady_of_Auxerre_Louvre_Ma3098_n2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
